      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate whether enhanced neuroproliferation could be involved in the pathogenesis of gallstone pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Gallbladders from 117 patients with gallstones and 43 controls were examined. The gallbladder samples were immunostained against the pan-neuronal marker PGP 9.5 and the number of nerves and nerve area per tissue area estimated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "More nerves and an increased nerve area per tissue area were found in uncomplicated symptomatic gallstone disease. In comparison, acute cholecystitis displayed a significantly (p=0.01) decreased number of nerves and nerve area per tissue area. In both categories, the gallbladder neck contained more nerves (p=0.06 and 0.04, respectively) and an increased nerve area per tissue area (p=0.034 and 0.008, respectively) than the body."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Uncomplicated disease showed enhanced neuroproliferation, significantly more in the gallbladder neck, whereas significantly fewer nerves were observed in acute cholecystitis. Nerve growth alteration may play a role in uncomplicated gallstone pain but the pathology may be different in inflammation."
        }
      ]},
